{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"046c2a5b5812b3e3","name":"card_event_plan_d","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/France/events/card_event_plan_d","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"PLAN D","text":"Choose one - your units get +2 attack this turn OR they get +2 defense.","flavorText":"Plan D, also known as the Dyle Plan, was a military strategy devised by the French high command in the late 1930s as a response to the growing threat of German aggression. The plan was primarily designed to defend against a potential German invasion along the Belgian border. It was based on the assumption that Germany would launch a similar offensive as it did during World War I, moving through Belgium to attack France. Plan D aimed to halt the German advance by establishing a defensive line along the Dyle River in Belgium, extending from Antwerp to the French border. The French Army, along with British and Belgian forces, would concentrate their troops in this region to counter the German invasion. The plan emphasized the importance of cooperation with the Belgian army and relied on the strong fortifications along the Franco-Belgian border. However, when Germany launched its invasion of France in May 1940, it did so through the Ardennes Forest, a heavily wooded and less fortified area that was considered impassable by the French high command. This unexpected maneuver bypassed the defenses established under Plan D and ultimately led to the swift defeat of France. In hindsight, the reliance on Plan D and its failure to account for the Ardennes offensive has been criticized as a strategic miscalculation that contributed to the rapid collapse of the French defenses and the subsequent occupation of the country by German forces.","faction":"France","type":"order","rarity":"Common","cost":3,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_plan_d","title":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_plan_d_title","SourceString":"PLAN D","LocalizedString":"PLAN D"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::France","kredits":3,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_plan_d_text","SourceString":"Choose one - your units get +2 attack this turn OR they get +2 defense.","LocalizedString":"Choose one - your units get +2 attack this turn OR they get +2 defense."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_event_plan_d_history","SourceString":"Plan D, also known as the Dyle Plan, was a military strategy devised by the French high command in the late 1930s as a response to the growing threat of German aggression. The plan was primarily designed to defend against a potential German invasion along the Belgian border. It was based on the assumption that Germany would launch a similar offensive as it did during World War I, moving through Belgium to attack France. Plan D aimed to halt the German advance by establishing a defensive line along the Dyle River in Belgium, extending from Antwerp to the French border. The French Army, along with British and Belgian forces, would concentrate their troops in this region to counter the German invasion. The plan emphasized the importance of cooperation with the Belgian army and relied on the strong fortifications along the Franco-Belgian border. However, when Germany launched its invasion of France in May 1940, it did so through the Ardennes Forest, a heavily wooded and less fortified area that was considered impassable by the French high command. This unexpected maneuver bypassed the defenses established under Plan D and ultimately led to the swift defeat of France. In hindsight, the reliance on Plan D and its failure to account for the Ardennes offensive has been criticized as a strategic miscalculation that contributed to the rapid collapse of the French defenses and the subsequent occupation of the country by German forces.","LocalizedString":"Plan D, also known as the Dyle Plan, was a military strategy devised by the French high command in the late 1930s as a response to the growing threat of German aggression. The plan was primarily designed to defend against a potential German invasion along the Belgian border. It was based on the assumption that Germany would launch a similar offensive as it did during World War I, moving through Belgium to attack France. Plan D aimed to halt the German advance by establishing a defensive line along the Dyle River in Belgium, extending from Antwerp to the French border. The French Army, along with British and Belgian forces, would concentrate their troops in this region to counter the German invasion. The plan emphasized the importance of cooperation with the Belgian army and relied on the strong fortifications along the Franco-Belgian border. However, when Germany launched its invasion of France in May 1940, it did so through the Ardennes Forest, a heavily wooded and less fortified area that was considered impassable by the French high command. This unexpected maneuver bypassed the defenses established under Plan D and ultimately led to the swift defeat of France. In hindsight, the reliance on Plan D and its failure to account for the Ardennes offensive has been criticized as a strategic miscalculation that contributed to the rapid collapse of the French defenses and the subsequent occupation of the country by German forces."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Common","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::BrothersInArms","isInPermanentPool":true,"chooseOneCards":[{"cardText":{"Namespace":"","Key":"EAB4ED304452D228EAC0509EB6BAD100","SourceString":"Your units get +2 attack until end of turn.","LocalizedString":"Your units get +2 attack until end of turn."},"selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":false},{"cardText":{"Namespace":"","Key":"556B49B1445DD5130D7713A4B883FF5D","SourceString":"Your units get +2 defense.","LocalizedString":"Your units get +2 defense."},"selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":false}],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'War_UI_Drum_Movement_Field_Snares_4'","O